.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-wallets-tokens-enable - Re-enable a holding the wallet already has


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 wallets tokens enable   [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Re-enable an existing, disabled holding.  is the token's symbol or 0x
address (add --chain-id when the wallet holds that symbol on several chains), or
the token id shown as token_id by 'rail0 wallets'. A token the wallet has never
held is not enabled here — use 'wallets tokens add'.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--chain-id\fP=0
	Narrow  to one chain when the wallet holds it on several

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for enable


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 wallets tokens enable 0xABCD… USDC --chain-id 84532
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-wallets-tokens(1)\fP
